DevOps Engineer Interview Experience – Wipro (3–4 Years Experience)
Just gave a DevOps interview at Wipro. Sharing the questions as I remember them, along with simple explanations 👇

👨💻 🔹 Started with basics
• Asked me to introduce myself
 👉 Just spoke about my experience with AWS, Kubernetes, CI/CD, and some real projects
• Then he gave a scenario like:
 “You launched an application on AWS, what will you do next?”
 👉 I explained setup like installing app, configuring server, security groups, domain, monitoring
 💡 Basically making it production-ready
• Another one was:
 “Your app is running on one server, how will you make it highly available?”
 👉 Load balancer + auto scaling + multi-AZ
 💡 If one server fails, users don’t even notice
• One tricky scenario:
 “My EC2 is in private subnet and I can’t access Google”
 👉 NAT Gateway + route table fix
 💡 Private server needs a path to internet
• Then he asked:
 “Okay NAT is done, still not working, what else?”
 👉 Check Security Groups / NACL
 💡 Sometimes issue is just blocked ports

☸️ 🔹 Kubernetes round
• “Explain Kubernetes architecture”
 👉 Control plane manages, worker nodes run apps
• “What is a sidecar container?”
 👉 Helper container inside same pod
 💡 Example: logging container with main app
• “DaemonSet kya hota hai?”
 👉 Runs one pod on every node
 💡 Example: monitoring agent everywhere
• “Where do you store DB passwords?”
 👉 Kubernetes Secrets
 💡 Don’t hardcode sensitive data
• “What are probes?”
 👉 Liveness = app alive
 👉 Readiness = ready for traffic
• “Taints and tolerations?”
 👉 Control pod placement
 💡 Only allowed pods can run on specific nodes

📦 🔹 Helm questions
• “What is Helm?”
 👉 Package manager for Kubernetes
 💡 Like reusable templates
• “Staging and prod ka difference kaise manage karoge?”
 👉 Different values.yaml
 💡 Same chart, different configs
• “Helm upgrade fail ho gaya toh?”
 👉 Rollback to previous version

🏗️ 🔹 Terraform
• “Loops in Terraform?”
 👉 count and for_each
• Scenario type:
 “I have DB + app + web created using Terraform. Need to create daily and destroy in evening.”
 👉 Terraform uses state file
 💡 It knows what to create or destroy automatically

🔀 🔹 Git scenario
• He explained a situation:
 “You’re working on feature branch, main branch has new changes, now you want to push only your work”
 👉 Rebase / cherry-pick
 💡 Clean and controlled commits

🐧 🔹 Linux
• “How to transfer a file >15GB?”
 👉 rsync / scp / split
 💡 Efficient large file transfer
